Title: PAPER STARS
Credit: An original short film
Draft date: 2026-09-29
Notes: Animated. No dialogue. Running time approx. 2:06.

FADE IN:

> PAPER STARS <

A single music-box phrase rises: five notes, climbing.

EXT. THE CITY - NIGHT

A skyline that never sleeps: glass towers, brick apartment blocks, water tanks, antennas. Thousands of windows. Warm kitchens, cold offices, the blue flicker of televisions.

Above it the sky is a flat brown-orange haze, completely empty.

A red light blinks slowly across it. It's only an airplane.

Traffic hums. Somewhere, a dog. Nobody looks up.

EXT. APARTMENT ROOFTOP - CONTINUOUS

MIRA (10), small, with a scarf that's too long, sits on the parapet of an old apartment building. A rusted water tower stands behind her. Her feet swing over a twelve-story drop she's long since stopped noticing.

A glass jar sits beside her, full of something small and pale.

She searches the sky, patiently, the way you'd look for someone in a crowd.

A blinking light. Mira sits up and POINTS...

...then sees it's the plane. She lowers her hand.

Her scarf moves in the wind.

INSERT - MIRA'S HANDS

A long strip of cream paper. She ties it into a loose knot and pulls it flat into a tiny pentagon, then wraps the tail around and around.

She pinches each edge with a thumbnail and the pentagon PUFFS into a small, fat, five-pointed star.

It starts to GLOW, softly, like a coal someone breathed on.

BACK TO SCENE

Mira cups the star in both hands and raises it over her head.

She lets go.

The paper star floats up, bobbing, until it hangs in the sky: the only star in it.

Mira watches it for a long moment, then looks down at the jar and unscrews the lid.

Paper stars pour out of the jar, dozens of them, and rise one after another into the wind like sparks from a campfire.

EXT. THE CITY - CONTINUOUS

The wind carries them out over the rooftops, a slow river of warm little lights drifting across the skyline.

In an apartment window, a SILHOUETTE comes to the glass, then another in the next building, and another. Someone points up.

A window goes DARK. CLICK.

Then another. CLICK.

EXT. THE CITY - WIDE - CONTINUOUS

It spreads. CLICK. CLICK-CLICK. CLICK. A ripple of darkness moves outward from the center of the city, block by block, tower by tower, faster and faster.

The orange haze drains out of the sky. The traffic hum fades away.

Finally everything is dark except for ONE WINDOW. It stays lit, alone, for a long, awkward moment.

CLICK.

Silence. Just the paper stars floating in the black.

Then a real star appears. Then ten. Then hundreds.

Then the MILKY WAY, a long river of light arcing over the rooftops, the way it looked for most of human history.

The paper stars drift upward to join it.

EXT. APARTMENT ROOFTOP - LATER

Mira leans back on her hands under a sky full of stars.

On the NEXT ROOFTOP over, a small FIGURE she has never met is looking up too. The Neighbor turns and WAVES.

Mira waves back.

A SHOOTING STAR streaks across the sky. They both point at it at the same time.

EXT. SKY - CONTINUOUS

We drift up and away from the rooftops into the stars.

FADE OUT.

> LOOK UP. <

> THE END <
